# Create slides with interactive 3D

<p class="hero-lead">This tutorial shows how to put interactive 3D models, typically made with OpenGL, in
your presentations: a model that turns, a simulation you tune, a curve you drag, live in the slide while
you speak. It goes through complete examples, from the simplest to the most advanced: each one with all its
code, to download, and a live demo.</p>

<div class="hero-actions">
<a class="btn btn-primary" href="01_tutorial/01_start/index.html">Start the tutorial →</a>
<a class="btn" href="code/interactive-3d-slides.zip">Download all the code</a>
</div>

## What you will make

A C++ program, compiled for the web, in a frame. The buttons below are outside it, as they would be in
the text of your slide: they change the program while it runs, and show a value it sends back.

<div class="live">
<iframe id="surface" class="demo" src="demos/c3/index.html?controls=ampl,k,speed&compact=1&panel=bl" title="A waving surface, a C++ program compiled to WebAssembly"></iframe>
<div class="live-controls">
<button data-demo="#surface" data-demo-set="shape=ripple">Ripple</button>
<button data-demo="#surface" data-demo-set="shape=waves">Waves</button>
<button data-demo="#surface" data-demo-set="drop">Drop</button>
<button data-demo="#surface" data-demo-set="ampl=0.25&amp;k=14">Strong</button>
<span>Amplitude: <span class="value" data-demo="#surface" data-demo-value="ampl" data-digits="2"></span></span>
